# The sha256 of the verbatim prompt block in prompt.md — the content BETWEEN
# the two ``` fences, nothing else. `evals/run_eval.py` recomputes it on every
# run and fails when it does not match.
#
# The fenced block alone, rather than the whole file, because the two halves of
# prompt.md have opposite doctrines. The block is the operator message this
# program came from, preserved byte-for-byte with its typos; it is immutable,
# and a revised or follow-up prompt becomes a sibling case (000a-…), never an
# edit here. The prose around it — the provenance table, the note on why the
# misspellings stay, "What actually happened" — is commentary on an outcome
# that was still unfolding when it was written, and it is expected to grow. A
# digest over the whole file would have forced a choice between never recording
# what the case turned into and re-stamping the digest so often that a
# mismatch stopped meaning anything.
#
# If this line and prompt.md disagree, the fix is a `git checkout` of
# prompt.md, not a new digest. The value of the case is that it is the exact
# input that produced this skill; edit it and the eval measures a prompt nobody
# sent. Re-stamping is correct in exactly one situation — the operator sends a
# corrected transcript of the same message — and that situation is a sibling
# case, which is a different directory with a different digest of its own.
b6236a098d4f09f77c5c4ce6824e323ca289762e977e1dc31161413f0d01de4b
